When the user presses Escape, Ladybird now exits fullscreen before
any keydown reaches the page, so the page can’t cancel it, and never
sees that keydown. That’s what Blink, WebKit and Gecko all do. In
WebKit, EventHandler::internalKeyEvent() calls fullyExitFullscreen()
and returns before it dispatches the keydown. In Gecko, PresShell::
EventHandler::MaybeHandleKeyboardEventBeforeDispatch() marks that
Escape mOnlyChromeDispatch, so content never gets it. And in Chromium,
ExclusiveAccessManager::HandleUserKeyEvent() takes the Escape in the
browser process, before the renderer gets it.

To decide if fullscreen is active, Ladybird checks the topmost ancestor
document in its process rather than the focused document alone. Gecko
does the same, with nsContentUtils::GetInProcessSubtreeRootDocument()
and that root document’s GetFullscreenElement().
